AB  - This article focuses on a systematic review evaluating the efficacy of nonpharmacologic therapies in managing postoperative pain in adults undergoing cardiac surgery. The review analyzed 23 randomized controlled trials involving 1,999 patients and found that therapeutic massage, music therapy, and hypnotherapy significantly reduced postoperative pain, anxiety, and stress, with massage and music therapy showing the most consistent benefits. Other interventions such as osteopathic manipulative treatment and electroacupuncture also demonstrated pain reduction but were less studied. The findings highlight the potential for these low-risk, accessible therapies to complement multimodal analgesia, emphasizing the important role of nurses in integrating evidence-based nonpharmacologic pain management strategies into postoperative care for cardiac surgery patients.
